The SHAPE of the result, which held across both specifications:

  - Income does essentially nothing for either chain; neither coefficient is
    distinguishable from zero.
  - The BA-or-higher share of the metro does almost all the work, and the
    Sweetgreen and Chipotle coefficients are statistically indistinguishable
    from each other. On the dimension you would expect to separate these
    concepts, they behave the same.
  - The one real difference is density: Chipotle's penetration rises with it,
    Sweetgreen's does not.

Say directly that you expected an income story and did not find one. A negative
result you can defend is worth more than a positive one you cannot.

Note in one clause that the log coefficients are elasticities, not
stores-per-million responses - do not quote the two kinds as if interchangeable.]

## The ceiling, and why I do not fully trust it

![](fig-ceiling.png)

[WRITE - open stores today, the ceiling under each specification, and the
1,000 target. Take the numbers from results/tables/ceiling.csv.

Report the RANGE across the log and level specifications rather than defending
a point estimate. Two defensible specifications disagreeing tells the reader
how much the answer rests on functional form, and that is information, not
embarrassment.

Worth telling as a short story: the first version of this model was linear in
stores per million, and it wanted to open restaurants in San Juan - a metro
where neither chain has a single store - because its income coefficient came
out negative and low income therefore read as high predicted density. Modelling
log density instead makes non-negativity structural rather than something you
clamp on afterwards. That is the same reason discrete-choice demand models use
exp(delta)/sum(exp(delta)) instead of a linear index. Be precise that this is a
borrowed functional form, not a demand system: store counts are chosen by a
firm, not by consumers.]

