## Caveats

[WRITE - the ones that matter:
  - a store base is a STOCK; it records where the chain has chosen to be, not
    where it could profitably be, so the regression measures revealed strategy
  - no opening dates, so "has not entered" cannot be separated from "entered
    and stalled" - the main thing this cannot answer
  - one cross-section, one scrape
  - income and education correlate at 0.55 across entered metros, so their
    coefficients should not be read independently
  - the store count is CHOSEN by the firm, and it is chosen higher where
    unobserved metro appeal is high - office density, lunch culture, rents,
    who is already there. That is the same endogeneity structure as price in a
    BLP demand model, where price is set higher where unobserved appeal is
    high. The instrument you would want is a cost shifter for opening a store
    that does not move demand, such as commercial rent per square foot. Name
    it even though you do not have it.
  - counts across markets are really the object of an ENTRY model
    (Bresnahan-Reiss, Berry), not a demand system - worth one sentence so the
    reader knows you know the difference
  - metro population is the wrong denominator for an urban-core concept]
